    "updated_at": "2022-05-03T14:07:15Z",
    "body": "> The conda module also looks at `CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV`. It works for me, but perhaps in your case the variable isn't exported for some reason? Try running `set -x CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV` to export it.\r\n> \r\n> You also seem to have some configuration for a different prompt in your config file, which could be interfering with starship—though I don't know why it would change the visibility of the variable.\r\n\r\nThanks for your advice, I've run `set -x CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV` to export it, but still not working.\r\nI can still check and see the CONDA_DEFAULT_ENV variable has changed to the new environment but the prompt isn't changing as the picture shows.\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/3250328/166468217-05440ce9-7611-45b2-8d9c-f0ca5822969d.png)\r\nbtw I also clean up my config file for my fish shell, here's my latest config file.\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/3250328/166468541-14d4f5d9-fc09-416f-9624-f320fbb74cd1.png)",
